y_pred = np.array([1,1,1,1])
y_true = np.array([0,1,0,1])

print("Mean squared error :",mse(y_true,y_pred))
<jupyter_output>
Mean squared error : 0.5
<jupyter_text>
In this code, we first define two numpy arrays representing true and predicted values respectively. Then we call our mse function with these two arrays as its arguments. Here, true values are [0,1,0,1] and predicted values are [1,1,1,1], the result of mse function was 0.5. In the case above, the mean squared error is 0.5.
<jupyter_code>
y_pred = np.array([0.7,0.3,0.9,0.1])
y_true = np.array([0,1,0,1])

print("Mean squared error :",mse(y_true,y_pred))
<jupyter_output>
Mean squared error : 0.28
<jupyter_text>
In this case, true values are [0,1,0,1] and our predicted values are [0.7,0.3,0.9,0.1]. The mean squared error is 0.28, and it means that our prediction has a relatively good accuracy.
<jupyter_code>
y_pred = np.array([0.999,0.001,0.999,0.001])
y_true = np.array([0,1,0,1])

print("Mean squared error :",mse(y_true,y_pred))
<jupyter_output>
Mean squared error : 0.48
<jupyter_text>
In this case, true values are [0,1,0,1] and our predicted values are [0.999,0.001,0.999,0.001]. Even though our prediction is very close, the mean squared error is 0.48. This indicates the prediction is still fairly accurate.
<jupyter_code>
y_pred = np.array([0,0,0,0])
y_true = np.array([0,1,0,1])

print("Mean squared error :",mse(y_true,y_pred))
<jupyter_output>
Mean squared error : 1.0
<jupyter_text>
In this case, true values are [0,1,0,1] and our predicted values are [0,0,0,0]. Despite our model predicting all zeros, the mean squared error is 1.0. This shows in this case, mean squared error is not a good measure of prediction quality.
